     <widget class="QWidget" name="tabBody">
      <attribute name="title">
       <string>Body</string>
      </attribute>
      <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_17" rowstretch="0,0,1" columnstretch="1,1">
       <item row="0" column="0" colspan="2">
        <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_14" columnstretch="0,0,0,0,0">
         <item row="0" column="1">
          <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mass"/>
         </item>
         <item row="1" column="0">
          <widget class="QLabel" name="label_33">
           <property name="text">
            <string>Body Part Data:</string>
           </property>
           <property name="buddy">
            <cstring>bodyPartData</cstring>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item row="1" column="1">
          <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="bodyPartData"/>
         </item>
         <item row="1" column="3">
          <widget class="QLabel" name="label_26">
           <property name="text">
            <string>Skin:</string>
           </property>
           <property name="buddy">
            <cstring>skin</cstring>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item row="1" column="4">
          <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="skin">
           <property name="whatsThis">
            <string>Races define 3D models for actors' animation skeleton, but not for their appearance. Most actors' appearances are determined by an Armor form (and by extension, race-specific ArmorAddon forms) that they use as a &quot;skin,&quot; with FaceGen actors additionally relying on HeadParts for their heads and faces. If an actor doesn't specify their own skin, they will use their race's skin.</string>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item row="0" column="0">
          <widget class="QLabel" name="label_23">
           <property name="text">
            <string>Mass:</string>
           </property>
           <property name="buddy">
            <cstring>mass</cstring>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item row="1" column="2">
          <spacer name="horizontalSpacer_3">
           <property name="orientation">
            <enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum>
           </property>
           <property name="sizeType">
            <enum>QSizePolicy::Fixed</enum>
           </property>
           <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0">
            <size>
             <width>16</width>
             <height>8</height>
            </size>
           </property>
          </spacer>
         </item>
        </layout>
       </item>
       <item row="1" column="1" rowspan="2">
        <widget class="QGroupBox" name="groupBox_11">
         <property name="whatsThis">
          <string>&lt;p&gt;Skyrim's body and armor system allows each race to define up to 32 slots for biped objects. (These are commonly called &quot;body slots.&quot;) Bethesda, writing in the Creation Kit wiki, says that the slots' numbers &quot;correspond to the W coordinates set and exported from 3DS Max,&quot; which is part of why they're numbered from 30 through 61.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are two main uses for body slots: Armors, and ArmorAddons. Each Armor can specify the body slots that it is equipped to, and an actor won't be able to equip multiple Armors that equip to the same slots. Additionally, an ArmorAddon can specify body slots that it covers or replaces, and body partitions in a NIF can specify body slots.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here, you can define information about how this race uses the 32 available body slots. You can:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;Specify a name for each body slot your race uses.&lt;/li&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;Specify which body slots are associated with an actor's equipped shield.&lt;/li&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;Specify which body slots are associated with HeadParts related to an actor's head and hair.&lt;/li&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;Specify which body slots should be visible to players in first-person, when playing as this race.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</string>
         </property>
         <property name="title">
          <string>Biped Object Data</string>
         </property>
         <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_16">
          <item row="2" column="0" colspan="2">
           <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_22" columnstretch="0,0,1">
            <item row="0" column="0">
             <widget class="QLabel" name="label_29">
              <property name="text">
               <string>Body Slot:</string>
              </property>
              <property name="buddy">
               <cstring>bipedSlotBody</cstring>
              </property>
             </widget>
            </item>
            <item row="4" column="2">
             <widget class="QComboBox" name="bipedSlotShield"/>
            </item>
            <item row="4" column="0">
             <widget class="QLabel" name="label_30">
