             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="3" column="0">
            <widget class="QLabel" name="label_25">
             <property name="text">
              <string>Unused Body Tex.:</string>
             </property>
             <property name="buddy">
              <cstring>bodyTextureF</cstring>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="7" column="1" colspan="4">
            <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="voicetypeF">
             <property name="whatsThis">
              <string>Specify the default voicetype for female actors of this race. Female actors can only use female voicetypes. If you don't specify a voicetype, the hardcoded AdultFemaleVoice1 form will be used.</string>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="6" column="0">
            <widget class="QLabel" name="label_27">
             <property name="text">
              <string>Decapitate Armor:</string>
             </property>
             <property name="buddy">
              <cstring>decapArmorF</cstring>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="6" column="1" colspan="4">
            <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="decapArmorF">
             <property name="whatsThis">
              <string>When an actor is decapitated, the game will cut their skin mesh at the neck, based on vertex weights. If that were all the game did, however, then you'd see jagged edges following vertex boundaries, akin to cutting a piece of paper along a dotted zig-zagged line. Instead, the game will force the decapitated actor to equip a special Armor (often called a &quot;gore cap&quot;) that resembles two bloody stumps: one on the neck side and one on the head side. Here, you can specify that Armor, per sex.</string>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="0" column="0">
            <widget class="QLabel" name="label_21">
             <property name="text">
              <string>Height:</string>
             </property>
             <property name="buddy">
              <cstring>heightMultF</cstring>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="3" column="1" colspan="4">
            <widget class="DKFormNIFPicker" name="bodyTextureF">
             <property name="whatsThis">
              <string>&lt;p&gt;This field is no longer used. It's a leftover from Oblivion, which used *.egt files to control FaceGen-related textures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In Skyrim, the baseline visuals for an actor's body are determined by an Armor form designed to be used as a &quot;skin.&quot; If you wish to modify a race's textures, you should edit the Armor and ArmorAddons that make up their skin. Note that it's common for multiple races to use the same ArmorAddons to provide their skins.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="2" column="0">
            <widget class="QLabel" name="label_99">
             <property name="text">
              <string>Behavior Graph:</string>
             </property>
             <property name="buddy">
              <cstring>behaviorGraphF</cstring>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="2" column="1" colspan="4">
            <widget class="DKFormNIFPicker" name="behaviorGraphF">
             <property name="whatsThis">
              <string>Specify a Havok behavior graph (*.hkx) file for actors of this race and sex. Behavior graphs contain animation definitions, allowing animations to accept parameters that vary at run-time and emit events that the game engine can react to. Race behavior graphs are sex-differentiated because per-sex behavior graphs can include or override animations in general behavior graph files, e.g. to provide male and female actors with different walk cycles.</string>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
           <item row="0" column="1" colspan="2">
            <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="heightMultF">
             <property name="whatsThis">
              <string>This value nominally scales the height for actors of this race and sex. In reality, it scales their entire size.</string>
             </property>
             <property name="alignment">
              <set>Qt::AlignRight|Qt::AlignTrailing|Qt::AlignVCenter</set>
             </property>
             <property name="value">
              <double>1.000000000000000</double>
             </property>
            </widget>
           </item>
          </layout>
         </widget>
